<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lithography">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithography</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lithography is a planographic method of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> based on the immiscibility of oil and water: an image drawn with a greasy substance on smooth limestone or a metal plate takes the oily ink, while the wetted, etched surface around it repels it. The German author and actor Alois Senefelder invented it in the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1796, and it was first used mostly for musical scores and maps. Godefroy Engelmann introduced multicolour printing, chromolithography, in 1837, and offset lithography, in which a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> cylinder carries the ink from plate to paper, has printed most books and magazines since the 1960s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithography.jpg" alt="Lithography" width="330" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre Puvis de Chavannes. Imprimerie Lemercier, 57 Rue de Seine, Paris. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centenaire_de_la_lithographie_par_Puvis_de_Chavannes_1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
